<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Hainaut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1293">1293</a>–<a href="/years/1331">1331</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Mahaut of Hainaut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of Hainaut was princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> from 1313 to 1318, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-villehardouin">Isabella of Villehardouin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florent-of-hainaut">Florent of Hainaut</a> and the last of the Villehardouin line. Married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-burgundy">Louis of Burgundy</a>, she was later deprived of the principality by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins of Naples</a> and died in captivity in 1331.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Matilda of Hainaut" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matilda_of_Hainaut_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matilda was born in November 1293, the only child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-villehardouin">Isabella of Villehardouin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florent-of-hainaut">Florent of Hainaut</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a> on condition that Isabella or any daughter would not remarry without royal consent. After Florent's death in 1297 the three-year-old was married to Guy II de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; Charles objected, but consented on 20 April 1300 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> had sanctioned the match.</p><p>In 1307 Charles revoked the rights of Isabella and her second husband, Philip of Savoy. Matilda, about fourteen, claimed the principality, but the bailiff Nicholas III of Saint Omer refused her and Charles gave it to his son Philip of Taranto. Guy died on 8 October 1308, and Philip betrothed the widow to his eldest son, Charles of Taranto, until in 1313 she was married instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-burgundy">Louis of Burgundy</a>, to whom and to Matilda Philip renounced his rights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Before they arrived, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-of-majorca">Ferdinand of Majorca</a>, who had received the claim of Matilda's aunt Margaret of Villehardouin, landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Glarentza</a> in 1315 and took most of the principality. Matilda came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Navarino</a> with 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, and Louis defeated Ferdinand at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manolada">Battle of Manolada</a> on 5 July 1316, where Ferdinand was killed. Less than a month later Louis died, some contemporaries suspecting poison by John I Orsini, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>.</p><p>King Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> then proposed that she marry his brother, John of Gravina. She refused, and in 1318 Robert's emissaries carried her to Naples by force, where she was made to go through a marriage ceremony and John took the title of Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. Brought before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, she still refused, declaring that she had secretly married the Burgundian knight Hugh de la Palisse. Robert used that marriage to deprive her of the principality and falsely accused her of a plot against his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She was held in the Castel dell'Ovo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and from 1328 at Aversa. Her cousin William I, Count of Hainaut, offered 100,000 livres for her release in 1324, and King Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> pleaded for her, both in vain. She died a prisoner in 1331, the last descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a> to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, and on her deathbed named as heir James, son of her old rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-of-majorca">Ferdinand of Majorca</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matilda of Hainaut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Villehardouin line ended with Isabella’s daughter <b>Matilda of Hainaut</b>, rival Angevin, Majorcan and Savoyard claimants divided the barons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catalan forces of Ferdinand of Majorca, claimant to Achaea, crushed the barons' levies and Burgundian knights loyal to Princess <b>Matilda of Hainaut</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-22">22 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Aversa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.97,14.21&amp;year=1317" aria-label="Aversa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Matilda of Hainaut"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 4011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 4011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.97,14.21&amp;year=1317">What was here in 1317? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
